# North America batch (1920-2024): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"na192063": dict(
 bluf="American suffragists, led by National Woman's Party founder Alice Paul and National American Woman Suffrage Association president Carrie Chapman Catt, set out to win the vote for women by constitutional amendment after 72 years of campaigning. The 19th Amendment was ratified in August 1920 and roughly doubled the electorate. It joined a wave of women's enfranchisement after the First World War; Black women in the South stayed largely barred from the polls until 1965.",
 now="Women now vote at higher rates than men in US elections, and the gender gap between the parties has become one of the largest divides in American politics.",
 story="The campaign for women's votes dated from the Seneca Falls convention of 1848, and several Western states, Wyoming first, already let women vote. National Woman's Party founder Alice Paul led pickets outside the White House and hunger strikes in prison; National American Woman Suffrage Association president Carrie Chapman Catt lobbied state by state. Women's work in the war industries of 1917-18 widened support. Congress passed the amendment in 1919 and Tennessee's ratification completed it in August 1920.",
 why="Seven decades of organisation gave the movement lawyers, lobbyists and a press. Western states from Wyoming onward had shown women voting without disorder. Women's war work made the denial of the vote harder to defend.",
 then="Women's votes split along the same lines as men's rather than forming the bloc both parties expected. Southern states kept Black women from the polls through poll taxes, literacy tests and intimidation until the Voting Rights Act of 1965. The League of Women Voters, founded in 1920, took up the work of registering and informing new voters.",
 fact="Tennessee ratified by a single vote: legislator Harry Burn switched after a letter from his mother."),
"d-scopes": dict(
 bluf="The American Civil Liberties Union wanted a test case against Tennessee's new ban on teaching human evolution, and Dayton teacher John Scopes agreed to be it. He was convicted and fined $100, a verdict overturned on a technicality, and the law stood for another 42 years. The trial, carried live on radio, made the contest between fundamentalist Protestantism and modern science a permanent fixture of American politics.",
 now="Arguments over teaching evolution continue in some US states and have broadened into fights over what schools may teach about religion, race and sexuality.",
 story="Tennessee's Butler Act of March 1925 banned the teaching of human evolution in public schools. The American Civil Liberties Union offered to defend any teacher who broke it, and Dayton teacher John Scopes agreed to be charged. In July 1925 defence lawyer Clarence Darrow faced prosecutor William Jennings Bryan, three times a Democratic candidate for president, and called him to the stand as an expert on the Bible. Scopes was found guilty and fined $100. The Tennessee Supreme Court overturned the fine in 1927 on a technicality and declined to rule on the law.",
 why="Fundamentalist Protestants held that evolution undermined the Bible and public morality, and had won laws in several states. The ACLU wanted a case that would reach the Supreme Court. Dayton's businessmen wanted the attention.",
 then="Bryan died in Dayton five days after the verdict. The law stayed on the books until 1967. In 1968 the Supreme Court struck down Arkansas's similar ban in Epperson v. Arkansas.",
 fact="The trial was the first in the United States broadcast live on radio, by WGN of Chicago."),
"na192964": dict(
 bluf="Investors had bid stocks up on borrowed money through the 1920s in the expectation that prices would keep rising. In October 1929 prices collapsed, and the losses, through margin calls, bank failures and policy errors, became the Great Depression: output shrank by about a quarter and a quarter of workers were idle by 1933. The slump discredited unmanaged capitalism, produced the New Deal and fed the rise of extremists in Europe.",
 now="It led to deposit insurance and stock market rules still in force, and its lessons (save the banks, keep spending up) shaped responses to the 2008 financial crisis and the 2020 pandemic.",
 story="Stock prices rose through the 1920s, much of the buying financed by brokers' loans. On 'Black Thursday', 24 October 1929, and 'Black Tuesday', 29 October, prices collapsed on record volumes. President Herbert Hoover assured the country that business was sound. Over the next three years thousands of banks failed and the economy shrank by about a quarter.",
 why="Buying on borrowed money had pushed prices far above earnings. Weak bank regulation let failures spread. Overproduction in farming and industry, and the war debts and reparations that distorted world payments, made the slump deeper and wider.",
 trig="Panic selling on 'Black Thursday', 24 October, and 'Black Tuesday', 29 October 1929.",
 then="The Federal Reserve let the money supply shrink and thousands of banks fail. Governments raised tariffs and defended the gold standard, which spread the slump worldwide. By 1933 a quarter of American workers were unemployed, and mass unemployment in Germany fed the Nazi vote.",
 fact="Shoeshine boys giving stock tips were a famous warning sign that the bubble had gone too far."),
"d-smoot": dict(
 bluf="Utah Senator Reed Smoot and Oregon Representative Willis Hawley set out to protect American farmers, and then every industry with a lobbyist, with higher duties. President Herbert Hoover signed the act in June 1930 over a petition from 1,028 teachers of economics; Canada and European governments retaliated, and world trade fell by about two-thirds between 1929 and 1934. The act became the standard warning against protectionism and the reason Congress handed tariff-making to the president in 1934.",
 now="It is invoked in every debate over tariffs, including the sweeping US tariffs of 2025, as a warning of retaliation and shrinking trade.",
 story="Farm prices had fallen through the 1920s. Utah Senator Reed Smoot and Oregon Representative Willis Hawley sponsored a bill to raise farm tariffs, and industries added their own products as it moved through Congress. President Herbert Hoover signed it on 17 June 1930 despite a petition signed by 1,028 teachers of economics. It raised duties on more than 20,000 goods. Canada, European countries and others retaliated, and world trade fell by about two-thirds between 1929 and 1934.",
 why="Farm prices had collapsed and Hoover had promised farmers relief. Once the bill was in Congress, every industry with a lobbyist asked for its own protection, and vote-trading carried the whole.",
 then="Canada raised its own tariffs within months and signed preferential trade deals with Britain in 1932. The Reciprocal Trade Agreements Act of 1934 let the president negotiate tariff cuts with other countries, which moved trade policy from Congress to the executive for the rest of the century.",
 myth="Smoot-Hawley caused the Great Depression. The slump was a year old when Hoover signed; one reading holds the tariff deepened it through retaliation, the other holds that trade was too small a share of output to matter beside the banking collapse.",
 fact="Both sponsors lost their seats in the 1932 election."),
"d-tva": dict(
 bluf="President Franklin D. Roosevelt and Congress set up the Tennessee Valley Authority to bring electricity, flood control and work to one of the poorest regions of the United States through public investment. It built dams across the valley, cut power prices and brought electricity to farms that had none. It became the model for state-led regional development that governments from India to Colombia copied.",
 now="The TVA still supplies power to about 10 million people as the largest public utility in the United States, and its model of federal investment in a poor region is the reference for every later regional development scheme.",
 story="In 1933 few farms in the Tennessee valley had electricity, floods were regular and much of the land was worn out. President Franklin D. Roosevelt signed the act creating the Authority on 18 May 1933, with Nebraska Senator George Norris as its champion in Congress. The TVA built dams along the Tennessee River and its tributaries, sold cheap power, controlled floods and taught farmers to restore the soil.",
 why="The Depression and the valley's poverty demanded jobs. The New Deal held that public power and planning could do what private utilities had refused to.",
 then="Within a decade most of the valley's farms had electricity. The Authority's power fed aluminium plants and, from 1943, the uranium-enrichment plant at Oak Ridge.",
 fact="Private utilities fought the TVA to the Supreme Court and lost in 1936 and 1939."),
"na193365": dict(
 bluf="President Franklin D. Roosevelt set out to end a banking collapse and put a quarter of the workforce back to work through federal action. His programmes regulated banks and markets, employed millions, protected unions and built a safety net; recovery was slow and full employment came only with the war. The New Deal made economic security a federal responsibility and built a coalition that governed for a generation.",
 now="It established the expectation, which no later administration has reversed, that the federal government answers for economic security, and it built the Democratic coalition that held Congress for most of the years to 1994.",
 story="A quarter of the workforce was unemployed and banks were closing when President Franklin D. Roosevelt took office in March 1933. In his first hundred days Congress passed laws that closed and reopened the banks, insured deposits, regulated the stock market and set up public works. Later acts protected workers' right to organise and, in 1935, created Social Security. Millions worked on federal roads, dams and schools.",
 why="The banking system was collapsing and a quarter of the workforce had no job. Voters had given Roosevelt a mandate for drastic action in 1932.",
 then="Output recovered slowly and full employment came only with war production after 1941. The New Deal coalition of workers, farmers, urban immigrants, Southern whites and Black voters dominated elections for decades. Many programmes excluded farm and domestic workers, who were disproportionately Black, especially in the South.",
 fact="The Supreme Court struck down two of the main early programmes in 1935 and 1936, and Roosevelt's plan to enlarge the Court in 1937 failed in Congress."),
"d-dustbowl": dict(
 bluf="Farmers of the southern Great Plains had ploughed up the grassland for wheat during the wartime and 1920s booms. When drought came in the 1930s the wind took the topsoil, and on 14 April 1935 a storm turned day to night across Oklahoma and Texas. Hundreds of thousands left the Plains, and the federal government took on soil conservation as a permanent task.",
 now="The Dust Bowl made soil conservation a federal responsibility from 1935 and remains the reference case for farming that outruns its climate.",
 story="Wheat prices in the First World War and the 1920s had drawn farmers to plough the shortgrass prairie of Oklahoma, Texas, Kansas and Colorado. Drought began in 1931 and the exposed soil blew. On 14 April 1935 a dust storm rolled across the Oklahoma Panhandle and Texas, turning afternoon to night. A reporter's account of 'Black Sunday' coined the term 'Dust Bowl'.",
 why="Ploughing removed the roots that held the soil. Years of drought dried it. Wind did the rest.",
 then="Congress created the Soil Conservation Service in April 1935, weeks after the storm. Hundreds of thousands left the Plains, many for California. Farmers who stayed adopted contour ploughing and planted shelterbelts.",
 fact="John Steinbeck's 'The Grapes of Wrath' of 1939 told the migrants' story and was banned and burned in Kern County, California."),
"na193566": dict(
 bluf="President Franklin D. Roosevelt and Congress set out to give the old and the unemployed an income that the Depression had taken. The Social Security Act of August 1935 created old-age pensions and unemployment insurance funded by payroll taxes. It became the core of the American welfare state and the largest item in the federal budget.",
 now="Social Security is the largest US government programme, and its projected funding shortfall in the 2030s is a recurring political battle.",
 story="Elderly poverty was widespread in the Depression after savings, jobs and family support failed. President Franklin D. Roosevelt appointed Labor Secretary Frances Perkins to chair a committee on economic security in 1934. The act he signed on 14 August 1935 paid pensions from payroll taxes on workers and employers and set up federal-state unemployment insurance. Farm and domestic workers were left out.",
 why="Millions of older Americans had lost savings and jobs. Louisiana Senator Huey Long's Share Our Wealth movement and Dr Francis Townsend's pension plan were drawing millions of followers, and Roosevelt wanted a programme of his own.",
 then="Coverage extended over the decades to farm and domestic workers, the disabled and survivors. Medicare was added in 1965. Automatic inflation adjustments were legislated in 1972.",
 fact="The first monthly cheque, for $22.54, went to Vermont legal secretary Ida May Fuller in January 1940; she lived to 100 and collected for 35 years."),
"w2-lendlease": dict(
 bluf="President Franklin D. Roosevelt wanted Britain to keep fighting Germany without the United States sending troops, at a time when most Americans opposed entering the war. The Lend-Lease Act of March 1941 let him supply weapons, food and vehicles to any country whose defence he judged vital, payment deferred or waived. It made the United States the 'arsenal of democracy', tied its economy to the Allied cause and sustained Britain and, from late 1941, the Soviet Union.",
 now="It became the model for supplying allies without sending troops, and its name was revived for aid to Ukraine in 2022.",
 story="By late 1940 Britain had nearly run out of dollars to pay for American arms. President Franklin D. Roosevelt proposed in December to lend equipment as one lends a neighbour a garden hose. Congress passed the Lend-Lease Act on 11 March 1941 after two months of debate. Aid went first to Britain and China, and after the German invasion of June 1941 to the Soviet Union. Deliveries came to about $50 billion by 1945.",
 why="Roosevelt judged that Britain's survival was America's first line of defence. Isolationist opinion ruled out troops; supply was what he could sell.",
 then="The Soviet Union received about 400,000 trucks, along with aircraft, locomotives and food. American industry converted to war production before Pearl Harbor.",
 fact="Roosevelt sold the idea at a press conference with the image of lending a garden hose to a neighbour whose house is on fire."),
"d-internment": dict(
 bluf="President Franklin D. Roosevelt, under pressure from the army and West Coast politicians after Pearl Harbor, ordered the removal of about 120,000 Japanese Americans, two-thirds of them US citizens, from the Pacific coast. They were held in ten remote camps, many until 1945, and lost homes and businesses; none was ever convicted of espionage. The internment stands as the reference case in American law and politics for what wartime fear can do to civil liberties.",
 now="Congress apologised and paid $20,000 to each surviving internee in 1988, and the Supreme Court repudiated Korematsu in 2018; the case is cited whenever a government proposes to detain or exclude a group on grounds of origin.",
 story="Japan attacked Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941. President Franklin D. Roosevelt signed Executive Order 9066 on 19 February 1942, letting the army exclude anyone from military areas; Lieutenant General John DeWitt applied it to all people of Japanese ancestry on the West Coast. Families had days to sell homes and businesses before being sent to ten camps, from Manzanar in California to Jerome in Arkansas. Many stayed until 1945.",
 why="Fear of invasion and sabotage after Pearl Harbor came first. Decades of anti-Asian agitation on the West Coast supplied the prejudice. Farm interests wanted the land, and politicians wanted to be seen acting.",
 then="The Supreme Court upheld the exclusion in Korematsu v. United States in December 1944 by six votes to three. Not one Japanese American was convicted of espionage. In 1988 Congress apologised and paid $20,000 to each surviving internee.",
 fact="The 442nd Regimental Combat Team, recruited from the camps and from Hawaii, became the most decorated unit of its size in US army history."),
"d-trinity": dict(
 bluf="The Manhattan Project, led by physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer under Army General Leslie Groves, set out to build an atomic bomb before Germany could. The Trinity test of 16 July 1945 exploded a plutonium device with the force of about 20,000 tons of TNT. Within three weeks the bomb destroyed two Japanese cities, within four years the Soviet Union had its own, and great-power relations have been conducted under the threat since.",
 now="Nuclear weapons have set the limits of great-power war since 1945. Fallout from the test reached communities downwind whose claims for compensation were still being argued in Congress eighty years later.",
 story="The Manhattan Project began in 1942 with Army General Leslie Groves in command and physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer directing the laboratory at Los Alamos. At 5:29 am on 16 July 1945 a plutonium implosion bomb exploded on a tower in the Jornada del Muerto desert of New Mexico with a yield of about 20,000 tons of TNT. Oppenheimer later said a line from the Bhagavad Gita came to mind: 'Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds.'",
 why="Fear that Germany would build a bomb first launched the project in 1942. By 1945 Germany had surrendered and the target was Japan.",
 then="Hiroshima was bombed on 6 August and Nagasaki on 9 August 1945. The Soviet Union, whose spies had penetrated Los Alamos, tested its own bomb in August 1949. The arms race that followed peaked at about 70,000 warheads in 1986.",
 fact="Oppenheimer named the test Trinity and said he had been reading the poet John Donne."),
"na194568": dict(
 bluf="The wartime Allies wanted a world body with the great powers inside it, built to avoid the League's failures. Delegates of 50 nations signed the Charter in San Francisco on 26 June 1945, with a General Assembly of all members and a Security Council in which five victors each hold a veto. The veto has often paralysed the Council, while the UN's agencies and Assembly became the main forum for decolonisation, peacekeeping and human rights.",
 now="The UN has 193 members. The veto has blocked Council action on Syria, Ukraine and Gaza, and Security Council reform is debated every year without result.",
 story="The League of Nations had failed to stop the war. Delegates from 50 nations met in San Francisco from 25 April to 26 June 1945 and drafted the Charter; Poland signed later as the 51st founder. It created a General Assembly of all members, a Secretariat and a Security Council in which the United States, the Soviet Union, Britain, France and China hold permanent seats and a veto. The Charter came into force on 24 October 1945.",
 why="The League had lacked the United States and any force of its own. President Franklin D. Roosevelt wanted the great powers bound in as 'four policemen', and the smaller states wanted a forum.",
 then="The veto paralysed the Council through most of the Cold War. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights was adopted in December 1948. Membership grew with decolonisation from 51 to 193, and armed peacekeeping began in 1956.",
 fact="John D. Rockefeller Jr. paid $8.5 million for the Manhattan site of the headquarters in 1946."),
"d-ironcurtain": dict(
 bluf="Former British Prime Minister Winston Churchill, out of office and with President Harry Truman beside him, set out to warn Western publics that the Soviet Union had closed off Eastern Europe and to call for an Anglo-American alliance. The speech at Fulton, Missouri, on 5 March 1946 was attacked as warmongering at the time and adopted as policy within a year. It gave the Cold War its defining phrase and framed the Western turn from alliance with Moscow to containment.",
 now="The speech named the division of Europe and marked the Western turn from alliance with Moscow to containment; the Truman Doctrine and the Marshall Plan followed within fifteen months.",
 story="Soviet forces held Eastern Europe and pro-Soviet governments were taking power there. Former British Prime Minister Winston Churchill spoke at Westminster College in Fulton, Missouri, on 5 March 1946, with President Harry Truman on the stage. From Stettin on the Baltic to Trieste on the Adriatic, he said, an iron curtain had descended across the continent. He called for a fraternal association of the English-speaking peoples. Soviet leader Joseph Stalin replied in Pravda that Churchill was a warmonger.",
 why="Soviet control of Eastern Europe was hardening while Western opinion still hoped for cooperation with Moscow. Churchill wanted the United States committed to Europe before Britain's weakness showed.",
 then="The Truman Doctrine of March 1947 committed the United States to containing communism. The Marshall Plan followed in June 1947 and NATO in 1949.",
 fact="Churchill had used 'iron curtain' in a telegram to Truman in May 1945; the Fulton speech made it public."),
"na195472": dict(
 bluf="NAACP lawyers led by Thurgood Marshall set out to overturn the 'separate but equal' doctrine that had licensed school segregation since 1896. On 17 May 1954 the Supreme Court under Chief Justice Earl Warren ruled unanimously that segregated schools are inherently unequal. The ruling gave the civil rights movement constitutional backing; enforcing it took federal troops and two decades of litigation.",
 now="Brown removed the legal basis of segregation and made the federal courts the engine of civil rights for two decades; schools segregated by income and neighbourhood remain the unfinished part of its work.",
 story="Plessy v. Ferguson of 1896 had allowed separate facilities for Black and white Americans if they were equal. NAACP Legal Defense Fund chief Thurgood Marshall argued five consolidated cases, led by that of Topeka schoolgirl Linda Brown, that segregation harmed Black children whatever the facilities. Chief Justice Earl Warren, appointed in 1953, assembled a unanimous court. The ruling of 17 May 1954 held that 'separate educational facilities are inherently unequal'.",
 why="The NAACP had spent two decades building cases against segregated graduate and law schools before attacking elementary schools. Warren wanted unanimity so the South could find no dissent to shelter behind.",
 then="A second ruling in 1955 ordered desegregation 'with all deliberate speed', which Southern states read as licence to delay. Arkansas Governor Orval Faubus blocked Little Rock Central High in 1957 and President Dwight Eisenhower sent paratroopers. Most Southern schools stayed segregated until the courts and the Civil Rights Act of 1964 forced the issue.",
 fact="Thurgood Marshall, who argued the case, became the first Black Supreme Court justice in 1967."),
"na195573": dict(
 bluf="Montgomery's Black residents set out to end segregated seating on the city's buses by refusing to ride them. The boycott, led by 26-year-old Baptist minister Martin Luther King Jr., lasted 381 days until the Supreme Court struck down bus segregation in November 1956. It showed that organised nonviolent economic pressure could beat Jim Crow and made King the leader of a national movement.",
 now="The boycott established the method, mass nonviolent action with economic pressure and a legal case behind it, that the civil rights movement used for the next decade, and it produced the movement's leader.",
 story="Montgomery, Alabama, required Black passengers to give up seats to whites. On 1 December 1955 NAACP branch secretary Rosa Parks was arrested for refusing. The Women's Political Council and NAACP activist E. D. Nixon called a one-day boycott, and Black residents formed the Montgomery Improvement Association with 26-year-old Baptist minister Martin Luther King Jr. as president. The boycott ran for 381 days, sustained by car pools and walking. The Supreme Court affirmed in November 1956 that bus segregation was unconstitutional.",
 why="Parks's arrest gave an organised community its case. Montgomery's Black churches, the Women's Political Council and the NAACP had the networks to sustain a year without buses.",
 then="Buses were integrated on 21 December 1956. King's home had been bombed in January 1956; he founded the Southern Christian Leadership Conference in 1957 and carried the method to Birmingham and Selma.",
 fact="It lasted 381 days, and King was 26 when it began."),
"d-moongoal": dict(
 bluf="President John F. Kennedy, six weeks after Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin's flight and five after the Bay of Pigs, wanted a contest the United States could win. On 25 May 1961 he asked Congress to commit to landing a man on the Moon by the end of the decade. Congress paid, NASA's budget rose to more than 4 per cent of federal spending, and Apollo 11 landed in July 1969.",
 now="'Moonshot' became the word for any goal with a deadline and the resources to match, and NASA's Artemis programme aims to return astronauts to the Moon in a new race with China.",
 story="Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin orbited the Earth on 12 April 1961, and the Bay of Pigs invasion failed five days later. On 25 May President John F. Kennedy told a joint session of Congress that the nation should commit itself to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade was out. NASA's budget rose more than fivefold between 1961 and 1966.",
 why="The Soviet Union led in rockets and in prestige. Kennedy's advisers, Vice President Lyndon Johnson among them, chose a goal far enough off that American industry could overtake.",
 then="Apollo 11 landed on 20 July 1969. At its peak the programme took more than 4 per cent of the federal budget and employed about 400,000 people.",
 fact="Kennedy told NASA chief James Webb in a taped meeting in 1962 that he was 'not that interested in space', only in beating the Soviets to the Moon."),
"d-hotline": dict(
 bluf="The Kennedy and Khrushchev governments wanted a way to reach each other in minutes rather than the hours the Cuban missile crisis had shown messages could take. In June 1963 they agreed a direct teletype link, working from 30 August. The hotline became the standing tool, and symbol, of crisis management between nuclear rivals and the model for every later one.",
 now="The link still exists, now over secure computer connections, and similar military hotlines are a recurring demand in US-China relations.",
 story="During the Cuban missile crisis of October 1962 messages between President John F. Kennedy and Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev took hours to encode, transmit and decode. The two governments signed a memorandum in Geneva on 20 June 1963 for a direct teletype link. The line, routed by cable through London, Copenhagen, Stockholm and Helsinki, began working on 30 August 1963.",
 why="Both governments had seen in October 1962 that a delayed or garbled message could start a nuclear war.",
 then="The Partial Test Ban Treaty was signed in August 1963, the first arms-control agreement of the Cold War. The hotline was first used in earnest during the Six-Day War of 1967.",
 myth="The hotline was a red telephone on the president's desk. It began as teletype machines in the Pentagon and moved to fax in 1986 and email in 2008.",
 fact="The first test message from Washington was 'The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog's back 1234567890'."),
"na196376": dict(
 bluf="Lee Harvey Oswald, a former Marine who had defected to the Soviet Union and returned, shot President John F. Kennedy dead in Dallas on 22 November 1963; his motive remains unknown because he was murdered two days later. The killing ended a presidency and shocked the country. Vice President Lyndon Johnson used the shock to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Great Society laws.",
 now="Distrust of the official explanation, from the Warren Commission onward, fed a lasting public suspicion of government in American politics.",
 story="On 22 November 1963 President John F. Kennedy was shot while riding in an open car through Dealey Plaza in Dallas; Texas Governor John Connally, in the same car, was wounded. Lee Harvey Oswald, a former Marine who had lived in the Soviet Union from 1959 to 1962, was arrested that afternoon. Two days later nightclub owner Jack Ruby shot Oswald dead in the basement of Dallas police headquarters on live television. Vice President Lyndon Johnson was sworn in aboard Air Force One.",
 why="The Warren Commission concluded in 1964 that Oswald acted alone. His motive remains unknown.",
 then="Lyndon Johnson invoked Kennedy's memory to pass the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and won a landslide that November. The Warren Report of September 1964 satisfied few; a House committee in 1979 found a 'probable conspiracy' on acoustic evidence later disputed.",
 fact="Kennedy was the fourth US president assassinated in office, after Lincoln, Garfield and McKinley."),
"na196477": dict(
 bluf="President Lyndon Johnson, civil rights leaders including Baptist minister Martin Luther King Jr. and a bipartisan majority in Congress set out to end legal segregation. The act signed on 2 July 1964 outlawed segregation in public places and discrimination in employment by race, religion, sex or national origin. It ended Jim Crow in law, committed the federal government to enforcing equal rights and began the movement of white Southern voters to the Republican Party.",
 now="It remains the foundation of US anti-discrimination law, and in 2020 the Supreme Court read its ban on sex discrimination at work to cover sexual orientation and gender identity.",
 story="In spring 1963 Birmingham police under Commissioner Bull Connor turned dogs and fire hoses on Black demonstrators, and the pictures went round the world. President John F. Kennedy sent a civil rights bill to Congress in June 1963. After his assassination President Lyndon Johnson pushed it through: the House passed it in February 1964 and the Senate broke the longest filibuster in its history in June. Johnson signed it on 2 July 1964.",
 why="Years of protest and televised violence against demonstrators had shifted Northern opinion. Kennedy's assassination gave the bill a martyr's cause, and Johnson's command of the Senate supplied the votes.",
 then="Segregation in public accommodation ended within months in most places. The Voting Rights Act followed in 1965. Johnson is reported to have said he had lost the South for a generation; Republican candidates won most Southern states from 1968 onward.",
 fact="The ban on sex discrimination was added by Virginia Representative Howard Smith, an opponent of the bill, and some took it as an attempt to sink it."),
"na196578": dict(
 bluf="Civil rights marchers led by Baptist minister Martin Luther King Jr. and SNCC chairman John Lewis set out to force federal protection for Black voters in the South; President Lyndon Johnson wanted a law that would do it. The act signed on 6 August 1965 banned literacy tests and sent federal examiners to register voters. Black registration in the Deep South rose within years, Southern politics was remade, and the act remains the ground on which voting rules are fought.",
 now="The Supreme Court struck down its federal pre-approval requirement in 2013 (Shelby County v. Holder); voting rules remain fiercely contested.",
 story="Southern states had used literacy tests, poll taxes and intimidation to keep Black citizens from voting; in Selma, Alabama, about 2 per cent of eligible Black residents were registered. On 7 March 1965 Alabama state troopers beat marchers led by SNCC chairman John Lewis on Selma's Edmund Pettus Bridge, and television carried the pictures that night. President Lyndon Johnson addressed Congress on 15 March with the words 'we shall overcome'. He signed the Voting Rights Act on 6 August 1965.",
 why="Literacy tests, poll taxes and intimidation had kept most Black Southerners from the polls for 70 years. The televised beating at Selma made federal action possible.",
 then="Black voter registration in Mississippi rose from about 7 per cent to nearly 60 per cent within a few years. Covered states had to seek federal approval for changes to voting rules until the Supreme Court set the coverage formula aside in 2013.",
 fact="John Lewis, whose skull was fractured on the bridge, represented Georgia in Congress from 1987 until his death in 2020."),
"na196879": dict(
 bluf="Escaped convict James Earl Ray shot Baptist minister Martin Luther King Jr. dead on a Memphis motel balcony on 4 April 1968, as King was turning the movement toward poverty and the Vietnam War. The murder set off riots in more than 100 cities and pushed the Fair Housing Act through Congress within a week. It ended the phase of the civil rights movement that worked through nonviolent mass protest.",
 now="King's murder set off riots in a hundred cities, pushed the Fair Housing Act through Congress within a week, and ended the phase of the civil rights movement that had worked through nonviolent mass protest.",
 story="Baptist minister Martin Luther King Jr. had turned to fighting poverty and opposing the Vietnam War and was planning a Poor People's Campaign for Washington. He went to Memphis to support striking Black sanitation workers. On 4 April 1968, the evening after his 'I've Been to the Mountaintop' speech, he was shot dead on the balcony of the Lorraine Motel. He was 39.",
 why="James Earl Ray, an escaped convict and segregationist, pleaded guilty in 1969 and was sentenced to 99 years; he recanted within days. King's family later questioned whether he acted alone.",
 then="Riots broke out in more than 100 cities, about 40 people died and federal troops were deployed in Washington, Chicago and Baltimore. Congress passed the Fair Housing Act on 10 April and President Lyndon Johnson signed it the next day. The Poor People's Campaign went ahead in May without him and faded.",
 fact="His birthday became a federal holiday in 1986, and his words are claimed across the political spectrum."),
"na196879b": dict(
 bluf="Sirhan Sirhan, a Palestinian-born Jordanian angered by New York Senator Robert F. Kennedy's support for Israel, shot Kennedy in a Los Angeles hotel kitchen on 5 June 1968, minutes after he had won the California Democratic primary. Kennedy died the next day. The killing removed the leading anti-war candidate, deepened the sense that the country was coming apart and helped Richard Nixon win in November on 'law and order'.",
 now="Kennedy's murder removed the one candidate who might have united the anti-war and civil rights coalitions, opened the way for Nixon, and shifted American politics toward 'law and order' for a generation.",
 story="New York Senator Robert F. Kennedy entered the Democratic race in March 1968 as an opponent of the Vietnam War. On 5 June 1968, after claiming victory in the California primary at the Ambassador Hotel, he was shot in the kitchen passage; he died on 6 June, aged 42. Sirhan Sirhan was seized at the scene.",
 why="Sirhan Sirhan, a Palestinian-born Jordanian, said he was angered by Kennedy's support for Israel; the shooting fell on the first anniversary of the Six-Day War.",
 then="Vice President Hubert Humphrey won the nomination at a Chicago convention marked by street violence. Republican Richard Nixon won the presidency in November on a 'law and order' platform. The Secret Service began protecting presidential candidates.",
 fact="Sirhan's death sentence became life when California abolished the death penalty in 1972; parole was granted in 2021 and reversed by Governor Gavin Newsom in 2022."),
"na196980": dict(
 bluf="NASA set out to meet President John F. Kennedy's 1961 pledge to land a man on the Moon before the decade ended and to beat the Soviet Union there. Apollo 11 landed on 20 July 1969, and astronauts Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walked on the surface while Michael Collins orbited. The United States won the prestige contest of the space race, and the programme showed what concentrated public investment in engineering could achieve.",
 now="Apollo spun off advances in computing and materials, and NASA's Artemis programme and China's lunar plans have revived a race to the Moon.",
 story="Apollo 11 launched from Florida on 16 July 1969 with commander Neil Armstrong, lunar module pilot Buzz Aldrin and command module pilot Michael Collins. The lunar module Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquillity on 20 July with under a minute of fuel to spare. Armstrong stepped onto the surface and Aldrin followed; they spent about two and a half hours outside. About 600 million people watched on television.",
 why="Kennedy had committed the United States in 1961 to a goal far enough off to win. Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin's flight had made space a test of which system was superior, and Congress paid for the answer.",
 then="Twelve men walked on the Moon by December 1972, and no one has been back since. The Soviet lunar programme was cancelled after four failures of its N1 rocket. The rivalry wound down into cooperation, from the Apollo-Soyuz mission of 1975 to the International Space Station.",
 fact="The Apollo guidance computer had about 4 KB of memory, far less than a modern calculator."),
"na197181": dict(
 bluf="President Richard Nixon set out to stop foreign governments draining US gold and to fight inflation before the 1972 election. On 15 August 1971 he suspended the dollar's convertibility into gold and imposed wage and price controls. The Bretton Woods system of fixed exchange rates collapsed within two years, currencies floated, and money has rested on trust in governments since.",
 now="Every major currency since 1973 has rested on trust in its government rather than on gold, which gave states freedom to print in a crisis and made inflation a political choice.",
 story="Under the Bretton Woods system of 1944 the dollar was convertible into gold at $35 an ounce and other currencies were fixed to the dollar. By 1971 dollars held abroad far exceeded US gold reserves, and foreign governments were converting them. On 15 August 1971 President Richard Nixon announced on television the suspension of convertibility, a 10 per cent import surcharge and a 90-day freeze on wages and prices. Fixed rates were patched at the Smithsonian in December 1971 and abandoned by March 1973.",
 why="US inflation and Vietnam War spending had put far more dollars abroad than gold to back them. Nixon wanted growth before the 1972 election, and Treasury Secretary John Connally wanted the burden put on other countries.",
 then="Currencies floated. US inflation rose through the 1970s and passed 13 per cent in 1979. The dollar stayed the world's reserve currency and oil was priced in it.",
 fact="Nixon made the decision at a weekend meeting at Camp David and announced it before the markets opened on Monday; in Japan it is remembered as the 'Nixon shock'."),
"na197483": dict(
 bluf="President Richard Nixon's re-election campaign broke into the Democratic headquarters at the Watergate complex in June 1972, and Nixon set out to cover it up. Investigations by the press, the Senate and a special prosecutor, and his own tapes, exposed the cover-up, and he resigned on 9 August 1974, the only president to do so. Congress limited executive power and campaign money, and trust in government fell for a generation.",
 now="Watergate remains the benchmark for abuse of executive power, and '-gate' became the suffix for every scandal.",
 story="Five men linked to President Richard Nixon's re-election campaign were arrested inside the Democratic National Committee offices in the Watergate complex on 17 June 1972. Washington Post reporters Bob Woodward and Carl Bernstein traced the money to the campaign. The Senate Watergate Committee learned in July 1973 that Nixon taped his office. The Supreme Court ordered the tapes released on 24 July 1974; one recorded Nixon directing the cover-up six days after the break-in. He resigned on 9 August 1974 ahead of certain impeachment.",
 why="Nixon's campaign organised the break-in. Nixon ordered the FBI investigation blocked and hush money paid, and the cover-up became the crime.",
 then="President Gerald Ford pardoned Nixon on 8 September 1974. Congress passed campaign finance limits in 1974 and curbs on presidential war and spending powers. Trust in government, above 70 per cent in 1964, fell below 30 per cent by 1980 (Pew series).",
 fact="The source Woodward called 'Deep Throat' was FBI Deputy Director Mark Felt, who admitted it in 2005."),
"na198185": dict(
 bluf="President Ronald Reagan set out to shrink government at home, rebuild the military and press the Soviet Union abroad. He cut taxes, deregulated, roughly tripled the national debt and then negotiated arms cuts with Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev. Conservatism became the governing creed of American politics for a generation, and the Cold War ended on his successor's watch.",
 now="Both parties still argue over his legacy on taxes, deficits and the role of government, and the Republican turn from free trade under Trump broke with part of it.",
 story="Inflation above 13 per cent, the Iran hostage crisis and a recession had sunk President Jimmy Carter. Former California Governor Ronald Reagan won 44 states in November 1980 on a promise of smaller government and a tougher line with Moscow. In 1981 he cut income tax rates by 25 per cent over three years and began a military buildup. From 1985 he held four summits with Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev and signed the INF Treaty in 1987.",
 why="Voters wanted change after a decade of inflation, defeat in Vietnam and the hostage crisis. Reagan offered a simple programme and the confidence to sell it.",
 then="The 1981-82 recession pushed unemployment above 10 per cent before growth returned. The national debt roughly tripled. Reagan's arms deals with Gorbachev eased the Cold War's end, and the Republican coalition he built held until the Trump era.",
 fact="Reagan survived a shooting outside a Washington hotel 69 days into his presidency and joked to his surgeons that he hoped they were Republicans."),
"na198786": dict(
 bluf="President Ronald Reagan and Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev set out to remove the missiles that could reach each other's forces in Europe within minutes. The treaty signed in Washington on 8 December 1987 banned all ground-launched missiles with ranges of 500 to 5,500 kilometres and let each side inspect the other. By 1991 the two countries had destroyed 2,692 missiles; the treaty was the high point of Cold War arms control, and its collapse in 2019 marked the unravelling of that system.",
 now="The US left the treaty in 2019, accusing Russia of deploying a banned missile, and Russia has since fired an intermediate-range missile at Ukraine.",
 story="The Soviet SS-20 and the American Pershing II and cruise missiles deployed in reply in 1983 could strike within minutes. President Ronald Reagan and Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev, after summits in Geneva in 1985 and Reykjavik in 1986, signed the Intermediate-Range Nuclear Forces Treaty in Washington on 8 December 1987. It eliminated every ground-launched missile with a range of 500 to 5,500 kilometres and allowed on-site inspection. By 1991 the two countries had destroyed 2,692 missiles.",
 why="Gorbachev needed to cut military spending and his reforms had opened room for agreement. Both sides wanted rid of weapons whose short flight times left no room for decision.",
 then="By 1991 the two countries had destroyed 2,692 missiles, checked by each other's inspectors. The treaty opened the way to the START agreement of 1991. The United States withdrew in August 2019, citing Russia's 9M729 missile, and Russia declared the treaty void.",
 fact="Reagan's rule for the treaty, 'trust, but verify', was a Russian proverb his advisers had taught him, and Gorbachev complained that he said it at every meeting."),
"na199087": dict(
 bluf="Investors and founders set out to capture the commercial internet after the web opened to the public in the early 1990s, and poured capital into companies with no profits. The Nasdaq rose fivefold between 1995 and March 2000 and lost about three-quarters of its value by 2002. The survivors and the fibre laid in the boom built the digital economy, which made the bubble the case for the claim that manias can fund lasting technology.",
 now="Today's tech platforms grew out of this era, and investors still measure new booms, such as AI, against the dot-com bubble.",
 story="The web opened to public and commercial use in the early 1990s, and Netscape's flotation in 1995 showed what investors would pay. Money flowed into internet start-ups, many with no revenue, on the belief that market share mattered more than profit. The Nasdaq index rose fivefold between 1995 and its peak of 5,048 in March 2000. It lost about three-quarters of its value by October 2002.",
 why="The commercial internet was real. Cheap capital, a long bull market and the belief that the web had rewritten the rules did the rest.",
 then="Trillions of dollars in paper wealth vanished and hundreds of start-ups closed. The fibre-optic networks laid during the boom made bandwidth cheap, and survivors such as Amazon and Google became the largest companies in the world.",
 fact="Pets.com went from IPO to shutdown in about nine months."),
"na199489": dict(
 bluf="The governments of the United States, Canada and Mexico set out to create one market; Mexico wanted its reforms locked in and the other two wanted growth. The agreement in force from 1 January 1994 phased out most tariffs, and trade among the three roughly tripled. Supply chains for cars and food crossed the borders many times, and the agreement became the symbol of lost factory jobs that fed the backlash against globalisation.",
 now="It was renegotiated in 2020 as the USMCA, and US tariffs on Canada and Mexico in 2025 strained the integrated North American economy it built.",
 story="US President George H. W. Bush, Mexican President Carlos Salinas and Canadian Prime Minister Brian Mulroney signed the agreement in December 1992. President Bill Clinton won ratification in Congress in November 1993 with side agreements on labour and the environment, over opposition from unions and from businessman Ross Perot. In force from 1 January 1994, the North American Free Trade Agreement phased out most tariffs between the three countries over 15 years. Car-making and farming supply chains spread across all three.",
 why="Leaders in all three countries expected freer trade to raise growth. Mexican President Carlos Salinas wanted to lock in market reforms against a future government.",
 then="Trade among the three roughly tripled, and supply chains for cars and food crossed borders many times. Small Mexican farmers were undercut by US grain. One reading counts hundreds of thousands of US factory jobs lost to Mexico; the other finds the net effect on US employment small and the losses concentrated in particular towns. President Donald Trump renegotiated it as the USMCA, in force from July 2020.",
 fact="The Zapatista uprising in Chiapas began on 1 January 1994, the day NAFTA took effect, in declared opposition to it."),
"na200190": dict(
 bluf="Al-Qaeda, led by Osama bin Laden, set out to strike the United States at home and provoke a war that would rally Muslims to its cause. Nineteen hijackers flew airliners into the World Trade Center and the Pentagon on 11 September 2001, killing nearly 3,000 people, the deadliest terrorist attack on record. The United States answered with wars in Afghanistan and Iraq, a surveillance apparatus and a global campaign against jihadist groups that defined two decades of world politics.",
 now="The attacks produced the security and surveillance apparatus Americans still pass through, two wars that ran to 2011 and 2021, and the 2001 authorisation for the use of military force that presidents of both parties have cited since.",
 story="On the morning of 11 September 2001 nineteen al-Qaeda hijackers seized four airliners. Two struck the twin towers of the World Trade Center in New York, which collapsed within two hours; one struck the Pentagon; the fourth crashed in a field near Shanksville, Pennsylvania, after passengers fought the hijackers. About 2,977 people died. Al-Qaeda leader Osama bin Laden claimed the attacks on video in 2004.",
 why="Al-Qaeda opposed the US military presence in Saudi Arabia, US support for Israel and US backing for Arab governments it called apostate. Bin Laden hoped a war would draw Muslims to his cause and bleed the United States.",
 trig="Nineteen hijackers seized four airliners on the morning of 11 September 2001.",
 then="The United States invaded Afghanistan in October 2001 and Iraq in March 2003, created the Department of Homeland Security, passed the Patriot Act and held detainees at Guantánamo Bay. The wars cost trillions of dollars and hundreds of thousands of lives. Bin Laden was killed by US forces in Pakistan in May 2011.",
 fact="It was the deadliest day for US firefighters in history: 343 died."),
"d-lehman": dict(
 bluf="Lehman Brothers had borrowed heavily to hold mortgage-linked assets and had little capital when house prices fell; Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson and Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke declined to rescue it. The bank filed for bankruptcy on 15 September 2008, the largest in US history, with more than $600 billion in assets, and credit markets froze worldwide. Governments rescued the banks that remained, and the rule that some institutions are too interconnected to fail was written into law.",
 now="Banks now face much stricter capital rules, and anger at the bailouts fed populist movements on both left and right.",
 story="Lehman Brothers, the fourth-largest US investment bank, held tens of billions of dollars in mortgage-linked assets as house prices fell through 2007 and 2008. The government had arranged the rescue of Bear Stearns in March 2008 and taken over Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac on 7 September. Over the weekend of 13-14 September Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson and New York Fed President Timothy Geithner failed to find a buyer; Barclays was blocked by British regulators and Bank of America bought Merrill Lynch instead. Lehman filed for bankruptcy on 15 September 2008 with more than $600 billion in assets. Credit markets froze worldwide.",
 why="Lehman had borrowed about 30 dollars for every dollar of capital to bet on mortgages, many made to borrowers who could not repay. One reading holds the authorities chose to let it fail to deter others; the other holds they lacked the legal power to lend against its collateral.",
 then="The insurer AIG was rescued with $85 billion the next day. Congress passed the $700 billion Troubled Asset Relief Program on 3 October after first rejecting it. The Dodd-Frank Act of 2010 raised capital requirements and created a process for winding down large banks.",
 fact="Lehman had survived the Civil War, the 1929 crash and two world wars in its 158 years."),
"na200892": dict(
 bluf="Banks, mortgage lenders and investors had bet on ever-rising US house prices through securities built on subprime loans. When prices fell from 2006 the losses spread through a system that had borrowed heavily and was lightly regulated; Lehman Brothers failed in September 2008 and credit froze worldwide. Governments bailed out banks while millions lost homes and jobs in the worst recession since the 1930s, and the anger fed populist movements on left and right.",
 now="The bailouts of banks while households lost homes fed the Tea Party and Occupy movements within three years and the populist politics of the 2010s; the zero rates and bond-buying used to fight the slump ran for a decade.",
 story="US house prices peaked in 2006 after years of lending to borrowers with weak credit, packaged into securities rated as safe. Bear Stearns was rescued in March 2008, the mortgage giants Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac were taken over in September, and Lehman Brothers failed on 15 September. Interbank lending stopped. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson and Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke won a $700 billion bailout from Congress in October, and governments in Europe rescued their own banks.",
 why="Subprime lending and the securities built on it came first. Heavy borrowing by banks and thin regulation let the losses spread. Global savings flows and low interest rates had fed the bubble.",
 trig="The bankruptcy of Lehman Brothers on 15 September 2008.",
 then="US unemployment reached 10 per cent in October 2009 and millions of homes went into foreclosure. Central banks cut rates to zero and bought bonds. The Dodd-Frank Act of 2010 forced banks to hold more capital, and the eurozone debt crisis followed from 2010.",
 fact="No chief executive of a major US bank was prosecuted over the crisis, a fact populists of left and right both cited."),
"na200892b": dict(
 bluf="Illinois Senator Barack Obama set out to win the presidency on opposition to the Iraq War and a promise of change, and the financial crisis of September 2008 carried him. He defeated Arizona Senator John McCain on 4 November 2008 with about 53 per cent of the vote and became the first Black president. His administration passed a stimulus, a health law and financial reform, and the polarisation of the two parties deepened through his two terms.",
 now="Obama's victory assembled a coalition of young, Black, Hispanic and college-educated voters that Democrats have relied on since, and the reaction to his presidency, from the Tea Party onward, remade the Republican Party.",
 story="Illinois Senator Barack Obama won the Democratic nomination over New York Senator Hillary Clinton in June 2008. The collapse of Lehman Brothers in September turned the campaign to the economy. On 4 November 2008 he defeated Arizona Senator John McCain with about 53 per cent of the popular vote and 365 electoral votes, and spoke to a crowd in Chicago's Grant Park that night.",
 why="The financial crisis and the unpopular Iraq War sank the Republican brand. Obama's campaign raised record sums online and mobilised young and minority voters.",
 then="Congress passed a $787 billion stimulus in February 2009, the Affordable Care Act in March 2010 and the Dodd-Frank financial law in July 2010. The Tea Party movement formed in 2009 and Republicans gained 63 House seats in 2010.",
 fact="Obama was the first sitting senator elected president since John F. Kennedy in 1960."),
"na201093": dict(
 bluf="President Barack Obama and Democratic majorities in Congress set out to cover the tens of millions of Americans without health insurance. The act signed on 23 March 2010 required most people to carry insurance, barred insurers from refusing the sick, subsidised premiums and expanded Medicaid; about 20 million more people gained coverage. It became the defining partisan battle of the decade and survived repeal votes and three Supreme Court challenges.",
 now="It survived repeated repeal attempts, and a dispute over extending its expanded subsidies was at the centre of the 2025 federal government shutdown.",
 story="Tens of millions of Americans had no health insurance and President Barack Obama made coverage his first domestic priority. The Senate passed its bill on 24 December 2009 with 60 votes; after Massachusetts elected a Republican senator in January 2010, Speaker Nancy Pelosi carried the Senate version through the House on 21 March 2010 without a Republican vote. Obama signed it on 23 March. It required most Americans to have insurance, barred insurers from refusing people with pre-existing conditions, offered subsidies and expanded Medicaid.",
 why="Tens of millions had no insurance and costs were rising faster than wages. Democrats held both chambers for the first time since 1994, and Obama judged the window short.",
 then="About 20 million more people gained coverage and the uninsured rate fell to record lows. Republicans voted to repeal it dozens of times and failed by one vote in the Senate in July 2017. The Supreme Court upheld it in 2012, 2015 and 2021.",
 fact="'Obamacare' began as an insult from opponents; Obama adopted the name in 2012."),
"na201695": dict(
 bluf="Businessman Donald Trump set out to win the presidency as an outsider against free trade, immigration and the political establishment of both parties. He lost the popular vote to former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton by about 2.9 million and won the Electoral College by carrying Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin by narrow margins. His victory moved US policy toward tariffs and scepticism of alliances and remade the Republican Party in his image.",
 now="Populist politics reshaped both US parties, and the Republican Party has become Trump's party.",
 story="Businessman and television host Donald Trump entered the Republican race in June 2015 and beat 16 rivals for the nomination. He campaigned against trade deals, illegal immigration and the political class under the slogan 'Make America Great Again'. On 8 November 2016 he defeated former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton with 304 electoral votes to 227 while losing the popular vote by about 2.9 million.",
 why="Discontent over lost manufacturing jobs and immigration was concentrated in the states that decided the election, and distrust of the political establishment ran across parties. Supporters saw a champion against an elite that had ignored them; opponents saw a candidate carried by grievance and by an Electoral College that overrode the popular vote.",
 then="US policy shifted toward tariffs, especially on China, tighter immigration and scepticism of alliances. Trump appointed three Supreme Court justices. He lost to Joe Biden in 2020 and won again in 2024.",
 fact="Trump was the first president with no prior government or military service."),
"na202096": dict(
 bluf="A new coronavirus identified in Wuhan, China, in late 2019 spread worldwide in early 2020 before most governments reacted. It killed millions, including more than 1 million Americans, shut down economies and moved work, schooling and commerce online. Vaccines built on mRNA technology arrived within a year and saved millions of lives; lockdowns, mandates and disputes over the virus's origin and the response deepened political divisions.",
 now="The pandemic fixed remote work in white-collar life, produced the largest peacetime government spending in US history and the inflation of 2021-23, and left public trust in health authorities divided along party lines.",
 story="The virus was identified in Wuhan in December 2019 and the World Health Organization declared a pandemic on 11 March 2020. Governments closed schools and businesses; US President Donald Trump declared a national emergency on 13 March and Congress passed a $2.2 trillion relief act on 27 March. More than 1 million Americans died over the following three years (CDC count).",
 why="A respiratory virus that spread before symptoms moved through global air travel faster than surveillance. Most countries had no stock of tests or protective equipment and reacted after community spread had begun.",
 then="Vaccines were authorised in December 2020, under a year after the virus was sequenced. Governments spent trillions on relief, and inflation followed in 2021-23. One reading holds that lockdowns and school closures saved lives at an acceptable cost; the other holds that the costs to children, businesses and liberty outweighed benefits that targeted measures could have delivered. The virus's origin, a market or a laboratory in Wuhan, remains unresolved.",
 fact="The Moderna vaccine was designed within days of the virus's genome being published in January 2020; the rest of the year went on testing."),
"na202197": dict(
 bluf="Supporters of President Donald Trump set out to stop Congress certifying Joe Biden's election victory on 6 January 2021, after Trump had told a rally that the election was stolen. Thousands broke into the Capitol, lawmakers fled and about 140 police officers were injured; Congress completed the certification that night. The attack failed in its aim and became the defining event in American arguments over the transfer of power, with the two parties disagreeing over what it was.",
 now="It remains a defining event in debates over the peaceful transfer of power, and Americans are deeply divided over how to remember it.",
 story="On 6 January 2021, as Congress met to certify Joe Biden's victory, President Donald Trump told a rally near the White House that the election had been stolen and urged the crowd to march to the Capitol, to make their voices heard 'peacefully and patriotically' and to 'fight like hell'. Thousands broke through police lines and into the building, forcing Vice President Mike Pence and lawmakers to flee; about 140 police officers were injured and one rioter was shot dead by police. Congress completed the certification that night.",
 why="Trump and his allies claimed the 2020 election had been stolen through fraud; courts rejected more than 60 suits and his own Justice Department found no fraud on a scale to change the result. His supporters believed the claim and came to Washington to act on it.",
 then="More than 1,500 people were charged. The House impeached Trump a second time on 13 January 2021 and the Senate acquitted him 57-43, short of the two-thirds needed. On returning to office in January 2025 he pardoned or commuted the sentences of nearly all of those charged. Democrats call the day an insurrection incited by the president; Trump and most Republicans call it a protest that got out of hand, the prosecutions political and the pardons a correction.",
 fact="Congress amended the Electoral Count Act in December 2022 to make clear that the vice president's role in certification is ceremonial."),
"na202499": dict(
 bluf="Former President Donald Trump set out to return to office on voter anger over inflation and immigration. He defeated Vice President Kamala Harris in November 2024, carried all seven swing states and won a plurality of the popular vote, the first Republican to win it since 2004. His second term brought sweeping tariffs, mass deportation efforts, deep cuts to the federal workforce and foreign aid, and court fights over the limits of presidential power.",
 now="Trump became only the second president to win non-consecutive terms, and his second term's sweeping tariffs and use of executive power are reshaping US trade and alliances.",
 story="President Joe Biden withdrew from the race in July 2024 after a poor debate performance and Vice President Kamala Harris took the nomination. Former President Donald Trump, who had survived an assassination attempt in Pennsylvania on 13 July, defeated her on 5 November 2024 with 312 electoral votes to 226, winning every swing state and a plurality of the popular vote. He became the first president since Grover Cleveland in 1892 to win two non-consecutive terms.",
 why="Voters ranked inflation and immigration first. Supporters held that the Biden administration had let prices and the border run out of control; opponents held that Trump's record and his conduct after the 2020 election disqualified him and that the result reflected a global turn against incumbents after the pandemic inflation.",
 then="His second term brought sweeping tariffs, mass deportation efforts, deep cuts to the federal workforce and foreign aid, and legal battles over the limits of presidential power that reached the Supreme Court. Supporters count the tariffs and deportations as promises kept; opponents count them as damage to alliances, trade and the rule of law.",
 fact="Trump was the first person convicted of a felony to be elected president; a New York jury had convicted him on 34 counts in May 2024."),
}
